* Intelligent Systems & Dynamic models

** The base model
*** 30 ECTS - Variable
    My assignments, including when which one drops and how hard each one is, scaled for a normal semester.
    Used for the task inflow - driving cognitive workload up.

    Uses PULSE_TRAIN for scheduled task drops. PULSE_TRAIN(start, duration, repeat_interval, end)

    @math
        &33 \cdot \text{PULSE TRAIN}(224, 1, 168, 1176) + \\
        &57 \cdot \text{PULSE TRAIN}(176, 1, 336, 1176) + \\
        &17 \cdot \text{PULSE TRAIN}(344, 1, 336, 1176) + \\
        &17 \cdot \text{PULSE TRAIN}(368, 1, 336, 1176)
    @end

    The formula represents my schedule from last semester, roughly scaled towards 30 ECTS. The start value consists of when it drops, 
    measured in hours deep into the simulation, + the offset (8am on monday = +8, tuesday +24 etc). A settling week before 
    the first assignments drop is added.

    The repeat interval is counted in hours too (168 hours = 1 week, 336 = 2 weeks).

    The prefix is the amount of load_units that's being dropped, eg how hard is the assignment.

*** 45 ECTS - Variable
    Is the same as 30 ECTS, just scaled differently.

*** Task Inflow - Flow
    The actual inflow of assignment tasks. Atm either uses 30 ECTS or 45 ECTS.

*** Cognitive Workload - Stock
    The main, most relevant stock. This stock displays the actual current workload of the student.

    @math
        Task Inflow - Completion Rate
    @end

    Initial value of 15, to display any carry-over workload from last semester, life management, whatever.

*** Completion Rate - Flow
    The completion rate defines how fast the student can drain workload from the `Cognitive Workload` stock, eg. how
    fast the student can finish assignments.

    @math
        (Cognitive_Workload/Workload_Drain_Time) * MAX(0.1, Energy / 100) * Is_Awake
    @end

    *Why?*
    - $(Cognitive_Workload/Workload_Drain_Time)$ basically defines pressure. This part increases the completion rate, the
    more cognitive workload the student has. That's because, on higher pressure, the student is forced to get more things done.
    - $MAX(0.1, Energy / 100)$ converts the available energy to a decimal, ranging from 0-1, with a safe-guard so negative can't have negative impact.
    - $IsAwake$ is just a safe guard, making sure that when the student sleeps, he actually doesn't drain workload.

*** Max Recovery Rate - Variable
    No matter how good recovery is, it can only get so good. Value of 10.

*** Clock - Variable
    Models a simulation clock - hour based - used for time-keeping, sleep and awake.

*** Hour of Day - Variable
    Uses the Clock to determine which our of the simulation day it is. Used for determining wether the student sleeps or
    is awake.

*** Is Sleeping - Variable
    1, if the Hour of Day is between 23 and 7 o'clock, otherwise 0. 
    We just lazily assume the student sleeps strictly from 23:00-7:00.

*** Is Awake - Variable
    Determines wether the student is awake. Calculated as the exact opposite of Is Sleeping.

*** Depletion Per Load - Variable
    Determines how much energy is consumed per workload. Currently set as 0.01.

*** Depletion Baseline - Variable
    Just living on it's own drains energy. Currently set as 0.5.

*** Energy Recovery - Flow
    How quickly the student gains energy again. Currently set to only gain energy when sleeping.
    The student gains more energy when he's more tired, less if less tired.

    @math
        \text{MAX_RECOVERY_RATE} * (1 - Energy / 100) * Is_Sleeping
    @end

*** Energy - Stock
    The main indicator of how much energy the student currently has. Calculated by simply weighing input vs output.
    The initial value is set to 75, while the spectrum is 0-100, so the amount of energy can be seen as percentile.
    
    @math
        \text{Energy_Recovery}-\text{Energy_Depletion}
    @end

*** Energy Depletion - Flow
    How fast the student loses energy.

    @math
        IF THEN ELSE(Energy > 0, (Depletion_Baseline + Cognitive_Workload * Depletion_Per_Load) * Is_Awake, 0)
    @end

    *In detail:*

    - $IF THEN ELSE(Energy > 0, ..., 0)$ - Just a safety guard making sure that we don't drain too much energy so that
      the student has negative energy
    - $Depletion_Baseline + Cognitive_Workload * Depletion_Per_Load$ - Adds the baseline (how much energy the student 
      spends by just living) together with the amount of work the student currently has, multiplied by the depletion per load.
      This basically means, each work unit the student has currently, converted to energy it takes.

    *Important note:*

    The more work load the student has, the faster energy depletes.
